'''Règim de compensació a l'exportació''' ({{lang|ca|Export Compensation Regime}}), commonly known as the '''negative tariff''' ({{lang|ca|arancel negatiu}}), is a [[Balboa]]n trade policy established in 1981 that provides rebates to exporters of agricultural products and manufactured goods. The regime is designed to offset the competitive disadvantage caused by the {{w|overvaluation|overvaluation}} of the [[International Dollar]] (ID), Balboa's currency, which makes Balboan exports more expensive than they would be under a hypothetical independent Balboan currency. The rebate is funded from [[Balboa Canal]] revenue and costs approximately $3–5 billion annually.

== Background ==

The International Dollar was adopted as Balboa's currency following the reunification of Balboa with the [[Adonian Community]]. The ID is a shared currency managed by the AC monetary authorities and reflects the economic weight of the broader AC membership, not Balboa's economy alone. For Balboa, a developing economy within the AC, the ID is significantly overvalued: it is worth more in purchasing power terms than a hypothetical independent Balboan currency would be.

This overvaluation creates a structural barrier to exports. Domestic production costs — wages, energy, transport, materials — are denominated in ID, and the strong currency makes these costs high relative to those in countries with weaker currencies. Balboan goods are therefore expensive for foreign buyers, even when the underlying production is efficient. The problem is asymmetric: the overvalued ID makes imports cheap (benefiting consumers and industries that rely on imported inputs) but makes exports expensive (penalising producers who sell to foreign markets).

The [[1968–1978 Balboa recession]] exposed the severity of the problem. With exports uncompetitive, the Balboan economy stagnated, unemployment rose, and the government was forced to sell off state assets. The recession ended only when the BPP government came to power and implemented a new economic strategy centred on domestic processing, the [[BTPMEU]] common external tariff, and the negative tariff.

== Mechanism ==

The negative tariff provides rebates to exporters of qualifying goods, effectively reducing the export price below what the overvalued ID would otherwise allow. The rebate is calculated as a percentage of export value, designed to offset part — not all — of the overvaluation gap: the difference between the ID's actual exchange rate and the estimated value of a hypothetical independent Balboan currency.

The rebate is funded from [[Balboa Canal]] revenue, which generates approximately $6.5 billion annually in tolls and fees. The negative tariff consumes approximately $4.2 billion of this revenue, with the remainder allocated to agricultural price supports ($1–2 billion) and other government expenditure.

== Scope ==

The negative tariff applies to two categories of goods:

* '''Agricultural products''': refined sugar, canned fruits, fruit juices, cocoa products, and highland coffee. Agricultural exports are the most acutely affected by the overvalued ID, as they compete directly with producers in [[Sinchew]] and [[Tapuya]] where weaker currencies and lower labour costs make production far cheaper.

* '''Manufactured goods''': assembled products (electronics, appliances, machinery), [[aluminum]] and refined metals, [[plastics|plastic resins and products]], and other manufactured exports. The negative tariff is particularly important for the assembly sector, which imports components from [[Sinchew]] and [[Tapuya]], assembles them in Balboa, and exports the finished products to Illypnian and other markets.

The negative tariff does '''not''' apply to '''petroleum products'''. [[CIEA]]'s refined petroleum exports — including the large COBAL contract supplying [[Balisca]] — are not covered by the regime. Petroleum competitiveness derives from a different mechanism: the overvalued ID makes crude oil imports cheap (the input-side advantage), and low hydropower costs from the [[Yalabirga]] dams keep refining costs down. The competitive advantage comes entirely from the input side, not from export rebates.

== Economic effects ==

The negative tariff is critical to the competitiveness of several key sectors:

=== Assembly sector ===

The assembly sector is the most dependent on the negative tariff. Without the rebate, assembled products from Balboa would be uncompetitive with those from countries with weaker currencies, and the supply chain would bypass Balboa entirely, routing components directly from Sinchew and Tapuya to final-assembly markets. The negative tariff, combined with the [[BTPMEU]] common external tariff (which protects the domestic market) and the overvalued ID (which makes imported components cheap), creates the conditions for the assembly model to function.

=== Aluminum ===

Aluminum production benefits from cheap hydropower from the [[Yalabirga]] rivers, which offsets most of the overvalued ID's cost penalty. The negative tariff closes the remaining gap, making Balboan aluminum competitive on the world market.

=== Plastics ===

[[Polímers de Balboa]] (PDB) sources petrochemical feedstocks from [[CIEA]]'s [[Badia Curta]] refinery at competitive prices (the overvalued ID makes crude oil imports cheap for CIEA), converts them into plastic resins and products, and exports with the negative tariff. The combination of cheap feedstocks, low hydropower energy costs, and the export rebate gives PDB a structural cost advantage.

=== Agriculture ===

Highland coffee is the most competitive agricultural export, as it commands premium pricing as a specialty product and the negative tariff closes the remaining competitiveness gap. Other crops — sugar, bananas, tropical fruits — survive only behind the BTPMEU common external tariff, supplemented by the negative tariff and government price supports.

== Controversy ==

The negative tariff has been a source of persistent controversy within the [[Adonian Community]]. Richer AC members — particularly [[Volisania]], [[Breisland]], and [[Okaiken]] — argue that the regime is a trade-distorting subsidy that gives Balboan exporters an unfair advantage over producers in countries with market-determined exchange rates. They contend that the rebate effectively lowers Balboan export prices below their true cost of production, violating the spirit of AC free trade rules.

Balboa contends that the negative tariff is not a subsidy but a necessary currency adjustment. The ID, Balboa argues, is overvalued for Balboa's economy — it reflects the economic weight of the entire AC, not Balboa alone — and the rebate merely corrects for this structural distortion. Without the compensation, Balboa argues, the overvalued ID would make all exports uncompetitive, creating a permanent trade deficit and economic stagnation.

The [[BTPMEU]] supports the regime because [[Tapuya]] benefits from cheaper Balboan assembly of its components. The BTPMEU common external tariff and the negative tariff work together: the common external tariff protects the BTPMEU domestic market, while the negative tariff makes Balboan exports competitive in non-BTPMEU markets.

== Fiscal relationship with the canal ==

The negative tariff is funded directly from [[Balboa Canal]] revenue, creating a tight fiscal link between the canal and the export sector. The canal generates approximately $6.5 billion annually, of which approximately $4.2 billion goes to the negative tariff and $1–2 billion to agricultural price supports. This means that the majority of canal revenue is recycled into export subsidies rather than being available for general government spending.

This arrangement creates both a political constituency for the canal — exporters depend on it for their rebates — and a fiscal constraint: if canal revenue were to fall (due to reduced traffic, competition from alternative routes, or geopolitical disruption), the negative tariff budget would have to be cut, which would hurt exporters and potentially trigger economic instability.

