Chemical products receive the largest sectoral share ($1,100 million, 26.2 per cent), led by plastics and resins ($460 million), which benefit from cheap CIEA feedstocks and hydropower but require the rebate to compete in non-BTPMEU markets. Agricultural products receive $1,100 million (26.2 per cent), with sugar ($340 million) and bananas ($260 million) the largest subcategories — these are "survival exports" that cannot compete with Sinchew and Tapuya producers without the rebate. Metals receive $670 million (16.0 per cent), with aluminium ($380 million) benefiting from cheap hydropower that offsets most of the ID overvaluation. Assembled products receive $530 million (12.6 per cent); the assembly sector is the most dependent on the negative tariff, as without the rebate the supply chain would bypass Balboa entirely. Semiconductor materials receive $400 million (9.5 per cent), reflecting the high value of polysilicon and fluorochemical exports to Okaiken and Balisca. Highland coffee receives the smallest agricultural disbursement ($50 million, 1.2 per cent), reflecting its inherent competitiveness as a premium specialty product.

